-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Microsoft Corporation — Visual Studio Code language configuration data
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What was taken
--------------
The DATA in `internal/langconf/data.go`: per-language comment markers, bracket
pairs, auto-closing pairs (including their `notIn` context lists), surrounding
pairs and folding markers. It is derived from the `language-configuration.json`
files contributed by the built-in language extensions of Visual Studio Code.

`internal/langconf/gen/main.go` reads those JSON files and emits `data.go`; the
transcription is mechanical and re-runnable, and
`TestGeneratedDataMatchesTheSource` fails if the committed table stops matching
its source.

Data only. No Visual Studio Code source code — TypeScript or otherwise — is
copied, reproduced, translated or derived from in this project.

Source, and which "VS Code" this means
--------------------------------------
This distinction matters and is easy to get wrong:

  * `github.com/microsoft/vscode` is the OPEN SOURCE REPOSITORY, licensed under
    the MIT License. The language-configuration files live there, and each
    built-in language extension's `package.json` declares `"license": "MIT"`.
    THAT is what this data comes from.

  * "Visual Studio Code", the branded binary Microsoft ships from
    code.visualstudio.com, is a PROPRIETARY BUILD of that repository, released
    under a separate Microsoft product licence. Nothing from that licence is
    claimed or relied on here.

The extracted files were read from a local Visual Studio Code installation for
convenience — the shipped app carries the same MIT-licensed extension files the
repository does, verified per-extension via the `license` field in each
`package.json`. Only extensions declaring publisher `vscode` and license `MIT`
were used, which is the set maintained inside the `microsoft/vscode` repository
itself. Extensions vendored in from other repositories — `ms-vscode.js-debug`,
for one — were deliberately excluded so that the provenance stated here is
exactly true.

